cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: "transport-runpod: the RunPod Serverless transport layer"
abstract: >
  A transport layer with no listening socket. A RunPod Serverless worker is a
  client of its own endpoint, so this takes jobs from a queue over HTTP and hands
  each one to an interactor as a command. Two workers come out of it: one whose
  interactor is linked in, and one whose interactor is another process reached
  over the iceoryx2 command bus. The queue protocol is undocumented by RunPod and
  was read out of its Python SDK's source.
authors:
  - family-names: Lee
    given-names: "K. S. Ernest (iFire)"
    email: ernest.lee@chibifire.com
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4570-1436"
version: 0.1.0-dev.1
date-released: "2026-08-17"
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric/transport-runpod"
keywords:
  - runpod
  - serverless
  - transport
  - iceoryx2
  - shared-memory

# What this repository is built on: the contracts it vendors, the library its bus is, and the
# source its protocol was read out of. Every entry is something it would not work without.
references:

  - type: software
    title: "contract-command: a command in, reply bytes out, and nothing about how it arrived"
    authors:
      - family-names: Lee
        given-names: "K. S. Ernest (iFire)"
        orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4570-1436"
    year: 2026
    url: "https://github.com/v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric/contract-command"
    notes: >
      Vendored as third_party/interactor. weft_interactor_t is the shape both
      workers here supply, and weft/cbor.h writes the replies.

  - type: software
    title: "contract-bus: the weft plane harness, the iceoryx2 C ABI as a dlsym dispatch table"
    authors:
      - family-names: Lee
        given-names: "K. S. Ernest (iFire)"
        orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4570-1436"
    year: 2026
    url: "https://github.com/v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric/contract-bus"
    notes: >
      Vendored as third_party/harness. weft/command.hpp decides the service
      names, the weft::byte payload and the 8-byte request-id envelope that
      include/runpod/bus_ask.h is the client half of; weft/limits.hpp decides the
      128 KiB bound on one message. third_party/harness/python/weft_harness is
      the same bus for the Python worker and belongs upstream in that repository.

  - type: software
    title: "iceoryx2: lock-free zero-copy inter-process communication"
    authors:
      - name: "Contributors to the Eclipse Foundation"
    year: 2026
    version: "0.9.3"
    license: Apache-2.0
    url: "https://github.com/eclipse-iceoryx/iceoryx2"
    notes: >
      The bus. Loaded by dlopen at start rather than linked, so nothing here has
      it in its build graph; the Python worker uses its PyPI binding, pinned to
      the same version the C ABI was transcribed from.

  - type: software
    title: "runpod-python: the RunPod SDK"
    authors:
      - name: "RunPod"
    year: 2026
    license: MIT
    url: "https://github.com/runpod/runpod-python"
    notes: >
      RunPod documents its client SDKs and its handler functions, not the wire a
      worker speaks. src/worker.cpp's protocol was read out of
      runpod/serverless/modules/rp_job.py, rp_http.py and rp_ping.py, and is
      observed rather than specified. python/ runs that SDK directly instead.

  - type: software
    title: "libcurl"
    authors:
      - family-names: Stenberg
        given-names: Daniel
    year: 2026
    license: curl
    url: "https://github.com/curl/curl"
    notes: "What the C++ worker speaks the RunPod webhooks with."
